---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import FlightDistanceCalculator from '@/components/tools/reference/FlightDistanceCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="flight-distance-calculator">
  <FlightDistanceCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the flight distance calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The flight distance calculator gives the great-circle distance between two airports: the
      shortest path over the surface of the Earth. It uses the haversine formula with the mean Earth
      radius <code>R = 6,371 km</code>:
      <code>d = 2R × asin(√(sin²(Δφ/2) + cos φ1 × cos φ2 × sin²(Δλ/2)))</code>, where φ is latitude
      and λ is longitude.
    </p>
    <p>
      On the flat map the shortest route looks curved, because the map stretches the areas near the
      poles. A flight from New York to London heads northeast first and comes in from the west.
    </p>
    <h2>Distance and flight time</h2>
    <p>
      Airlines fly a little further than the great-circle distance because of air routes, wind and
      approach paths. The flight time estimate assumes an average 800 km/h plus 30 minutes for taxi,
      climb and descent. Jet streams can change it by an hour on long flights.
    </p>
    <h2>Miles and nautical miles</h2>
    <p>
      One mile is 1.609344 km; one nautical mile is 1.852 km, one minute of latitude. Aviation uses
      nautical miles; frequent-flyer programmes often count statute miles.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
